// 0.179.18 (12/11): **a demo for the panel of the Matrix, and the first app of this tree with no page at all**. The owner:
// *«fes una app de demo per l'M5Atom Matrix: Leds multicolor com l'arc iris i amb canvis de to suaus i movimient»*. It is
// `apps/rainbow`, and it is written for a board where the light **is** the face (docs/40, docs/50):
//   - **the panel comes from the count and not from a constant**: `led.caps().count` is twenty-five and the square of it is
//     the five by five, so the same app on the Lite's one light is a hue that breathes —`rainbow: 1 light(s) in 1 by 1`—
//     and a board of nine lights would be a three by three. Nothing of the app knows it is running on the Matrix except the
//     number the board gave it (docs/30);
//   - **four fields take turns —bands, iris, spiral, rain— and the change is a mix and not a cut**: the field that leaves and
//     the one that arrives are mixed over two and a half seconds in hue and in level, and the hue goes the **short way round
//     the wheel** —the difference is folded into minus a half and a half—, so the two colours a person sees between are the
//     neighbours of the wheel and not a muddy average in RGB. Over that, a breath of nine percent once in eleven seconds is
//     the tone that never stops moving. Measured on the window (0.179.17's band, `--board m5stack-atom-matrix --gui`): the
//     four fields take their turn every nine and a half seconds, which is `kHoldMs` plus `kFadeMs` and the log agrees
//     (`[rainbow] field 2 of 4: iris`), and the app holds its forty milliseconds a frame while doing it;
//   - **the level is the board's own ceiling**: `led.caps().max` is twenty on this panel —twenty-five 2020 LEDs in a closed
//     case heat up above it— and a ramp written against 255 would be a ramp the system silently cuts. The frame is at most
//     (20, 20, 20) and the window says so: this is a demo that is as bright as the maker allows and not one that lies about
//     it (docs/30, docs/50);
//   - **a frame is one turn and the turn ends in a wait**: `sys.delay(40):await()` gives the coroutine back, the kernel
//     latches the strip at the end of the turn, and the twenty-five lights are computed (+two fields and a mix each) in
//     about three thousand instructions of the twenty thousand a turn has, so a panel is never sent half painted (docs/20,
//     docs/75). The cost of the wait is the app's own: the delay is a promise and the kernel keeps turning while it waits;
//   - **the one button jumps to the boundary between two fields**, which is the only instant where the frame has no mix in
//     it: `bias` moves the app's clock by `kCycleMs` minus the phase it is at, so the field after the press is the next one
//     whole and not half of a mix. It is read with `button.down(0)` once a frame, which is the third of the three ways the
//     docs give (docs/30);
//   - **and the case of the suite reads the panel and not the app's intentions** (`test_the_rainbow_app_paints_the_panel_of_
//     the_matrix`, `test_apps`): a model of the Matrix, the app of the repository copied onto the disk of the suite, and then
//     the twenty-five lights of the strip: every light is lit, there are six or more colours and none over the ceiling, four
//     hundred milliseconds of the app's clock leave lights where they were, the press is answered with `field 2 of 4: iris`,
//     and closing the app puts all twenty-five out (docs/85);
//   - **what the case taught about the harness**: `emu::advance_ms` moves the **board's** clock —the one `led` and the alarms
//     read— and `sys.time_ms` is `pal::system().millis()`, which on the PC is the host's real counter. An app's frame is
//     therefore waited for in turns —one turn is one millisecond— and not jumped with `advance_ms`, and the case says it in
//     a comment so the next one does not lose an afternoon to it (docs/85);
//   - **the icon is its own**: five by five dots with the wheel laid diagonally as greys, drawn at `res/icon-source.png` and
//     taken down with `tools/make_icon.py`, because this is an app a person runs and not a test app with the lunokhod
//     (`apps/luaperf` is the test one, docs/40);
//   - **it is not on the image of any board**: no board carries apps (`custom_apps_in_disk = no`), so it travels on a card
//     or by `custom_apps = rainbow` on the environment, and on the PC it is already on the disk the emulator opens
//     (`run rainbow`, or `--run rainbow` at boot). Its `boards` is the two ATOMs, so the Paper lists it and refuses it
//     (docs/40, docs/70, docs/85).

// 0.179.17 (12/11): **the lights of the board are part of the window**. The owner: *«l'emulador d'atom matrix i d'atom lite
// han de tenir els leds corresponents a la seva finestra del emulador (ja tenen el botó) tal i com es fa amb el m5paper»*.
// The Matrix's twenty-five WS2812s in a five by five and the Lite's one are the whole face of those boards, and the window
// was showing their button and nothing else:
//   - **the window has three parts now** —the glass, the band of lights and the keys— and the band is **under the page and
//     above the row of keys**, which is the order of the board's own face; a board with no glass has it at the top, and a
//     model with no light (the Paper) has no band. The geometry is pure and has proof in `test_ui`: five lights a row and as
//     many rows as it takes, twelve window pixels a light, four of air and six of margin, so twenty-five is the panel of the
//     Matrix and one is a dot;
//   - **the page and the band share the *stage***, the window without the strip of the keys: the first attempt centred the
//     band in the whole window, and the Matrix opened 76 by 88 with the button drawn **over** the panel (found on the window,
//     12/11). The stage is also at least as tall as a column of keys, or a board of one light and one key opened a window of
//     twenty-four pixels with a button of forty hanging out of it;
//   - **the colour is the frame the strip latched**, not what the kernel is animating: `FakeLed::show()` is what sends it,
//     the same rule the glass follows. An off light is a very dark grey and not the case, so the five by five reads as a
//     panel; the window numbers the lights row by row from the top left, which is the number `led.pixel` takes, and the
//     emulator keeps the board's ceiling —a `led ff0000` is (20, 0, 0) here too—;
//   - **and a board of lights alone has a window**: the rule that opens one looked at the keys, so the T-Koala —no key and a
//     green LED— had none and its light was nowhere. The window is what the board has: glass, lights, keys, in whichever
//     combination (docs/85);
//   - measured on the window under Xvfb, both backends: the Matrix 128 by 88 with 25 dots and the button on its right, the
//     Lite 64 by 40 with one dot, the Koala 12 by 24 with one dot and no key, the Paper 322 by 480 at scale 2 with no band
//     (`107` of `test_ui`, the suite green, the six environments built).

// 0.179.16 (11/11): **a partial paint asks for the box of the change, and a cell of the launcher does not flash**.
//   - **The rectangle**: the driver forced every partial paint to start at the left edge of the glass, a rule read off
//     the log of 0.118.6 —the splash and the bar went up and every other paint showed nothing— on a night that had a
//     second fault in it (the panel was never selected again after the splash, 0.118.9), so every paint after the first
//     one failed whatever its rectangle was. **The paper project is the proof**: it has been painting arbitrary boxes
//     on this same controller for weeks with `LD_IMG_AREA` and `UpdateArea` carrying the real rectangle, and its only
//     precaution is rounding to four (`src/plat/m5epd_hal.cpp`). The area is the box of the change now, with both axes
//     rounded to four the way that driver rounds them. It matters because the page is turned ninety degrees: the forced
//     edge was the **top of the page**, so one cell of a launcher cost a **column** —168 px for the first row and 652
//     for the last, against the 107 the cell has (measured on the board, 11/11)—. `panel [edge|box]` says what the
//     glass does and changes it, and `edge` is the way back without a flash;
//   - **a cell of the launcher does not flash**: on the board the grid blinked once per cell —`paint 24,12 242x107 mode
//     2 gc16`, ten a visit— and the question was the right one: **is the strongest wash really needed for white and
//     black text?** It is not. `lib/ttf` fills a glyph with the winding rule and writes
//     `rows[row * row_bytes + (x >> 3)] |= 0x80u >> (x & 7)`: **the text of the system is one bit a pixel**, level 0 and
//     level 15 and nothing between, so there are no greys for a wash to land nor to clean. `quality_of_the_page()` sent
//     a page of one bit to `gc16` all the same —four blinks— once the budget of 32 painted boxes was spent, and the grid
//     spends ten of those a visit. A page whose ink is only levels 0 and 15 answers `gl16` now, and the launcher paints
//     the picture and the letter of a cell in `gld16`: **a cell of the grid does not blink at all**;
//   - the prices, for the record: `du` is 120 ms and leaves 5 of 16 of what was there, `gl16` and `gc16` are 450 ms
//     with one blink and with four, and `gld16` is 450 ms with none (docs/50, docs/85). One case measures it:
//     `test_a_page_of_one_bit_is_never_sent_to_the_full_wash` paints a line forty times and reads the quality of every
//     paint —thirty-nine of them used to be `gc16`—.
//
// **And the first game of the paper project is ported**: `2048` (app 0.1.0), with its board, its swipe, its two lines
// and its `New` button that asks «Sure?» before it throws a game away, the part game kept in
// `/data/2048/game.txt` and its icon from the master of the other project. Three things of it are this system's and not
// that one's, and they are the whole of what the port taught: **the swipe is the `drag` gesture** —`dx` and `dy`, thirty
// pixels of threshold— because `swipe_left` and its three brothers are not gestures here; **the quality is an argument
// of the drawing call** and not a `damage()` of its own; and **the button and the two lines are controls of the system**
// —`ui.button` with `focusable = false` and two `ui.label`, which are opaque and take the old number away with their own
// box—, while the board of sixteen cells is painted by hand, a box at a time, and **a cell that empties is paper first
// and a frame after**: the paper's `on_draw` clears the whole view every time it draws and this page does not, so the
// paper of a box is the app's job, and without it an emptied cell kept its tile inside the frame and the next tile that
// landed there found it again (found on the board, 11/11).
//
// **And the Paper carries every app and no longer a list of them**: `[env:m5-paper]` had `custom_apps` written by hand
// —eleven names, one per app that had arrived— and the twelve now in `apps/` did not fit in a list that nobody
// updated: the ported game stayed out of the image and the launcher of the board did not have it. It is the second
// time in a day that that list cost a disk (0.178.7 left it empty and `run home_eink` answered `no such app`), so the
// list is gone and the filter that `docs/70` already documents is the rule: `custom_apps_in_disk = yes` carries every
// folder whose manifest names this board or its family, and an app that names another board says why at build time.

// 0.179.15 (11/11): **a splash is the plane of the glass and not a picture**. A `boot-*.bmp` of 540 by 960 at eight
// bits is **519 478 B** and the panel has four, so the old path read the whole file, converted 518 400 pixels one at a
// time —the 601 luminance and the threshold of a four-by-four ordered dither each—, wrote a second block of 246 240 B
// and blitted it. The disk carries a **`.e4`** now: four bits a pixel, one byte for two, the left one in the high
// nibble, `width/2` bytes a row and **no header at all**, which is the packing of the controller's own image.
//   - **the load is one block and one blit**: 259 200 B of the glass —540 by 960, the bar included and painted over
//     it after— read into extra memory and copied to the panel's image a row at a time, with no decode, no second
//     buffer and no wash —the blit covers every pixel—;
//   - **`tools/e4pack.py`** is what makes one, and its arithmetic is the decoder's and not a second: the 601 luminance
//     `(77r + 150g + 29b + 128) >> 8` and the ordered dither `(grey * 15 + bayer[y & 3][x & 3] * 16) / 255`, so a
//     picture packed there is the picture the system would have decoded, pixel for pixel;
//   - **`make_disk` packs them at every build of a board with a glass** and leaves the drawing out of the image: the
//     three pictures of the boot go from **1 558 434 B to 777 600 B**, and what reaches the controller for each one
//     goes from 519 478 B read and decoded to 259 200 B copied. A picture the tool refuses —a PNG, a compressed
//     BMP— **stays as it is** and the system decodes it the way it always did, so a folder of somebody's drawings is
//     never left without a picture;
//   - **`e4` is the first extension of the list**, so a disk that carries the drawing and the plane reads the plane;
//     and a plane that is not the size of this glass is refused with the reason —`the plane is not the size of this
//     glass`— instead of being painted in a corner (docs/30, docs/50).
// What is not measured yet is the boot on the board: the sizes are, and the milliseconds of the decode are the one
// number that wants the glass in hand.

// 0.179.14 (11/11): **and the sample book does not go to a board with no screen either**. The 0.179.13 left the
// pictures of the boot and the faces of the text out of the image of a board whose identity says `LUNOK_GLASS 0` and
// kept `epub/la-vaca-cega.epub` in it, which is 4 845 B of a book that only the reader opens —and the reader is
// `book.cpp`, which that build does not compile—: a file nobody on that board can read. It goes with the other two
// now, because a rule that leaves two of the three out is a rule nobody can explain, and the line of the disk says
// all three: `…, 7 files of the boot, of the faces and of the sample book left out (no glass)`. The disk of a 4 MB
// board with no glass is `data/` and the three sample files of `res/`, about 1 900 B (docs/70, docs/85).

// 0.179.13 (11/11): **a board with no glass is not given the pictures of the boot nor the faces of the text**. The
// build of the ATOM stopped with `lfs warn:475: No more free space 548` and the line under it told the person to carry
// fewer apps with `custom_apps`, which is what that environment already does: the four boards without a panel carry no
// app and no home app (`custom_apps_in_disk = no`, `-DLUNOK_HOME_APP` empty, checked). What did not fit was the
// system's own content, measured: `data/` is **1 893 921 B in 32 files**, and of those, `res/splash` is **1 558 434 B**
// (three `boot-*.bmp` of 519 478 B) and `res/fonts` **315 805 B**, against the **1 441 792 B** of LittleFS that
// `partitions.csv` gives a 4 MB board — the pictures alone are more than the whole partition.
//   - `make_disk` reads `LUNOK_GLASS` of the board's own `board/identity.h` and, when it is 0, **leaves `res/splash` and
//     `res/fonts` out of the image**: the two folders are opened by `splash.cpp` and `font.cpp`, which that build does
//     not compile at all. What is left is `res/` with the three sample files, `epub/` and `data/`, about 6 800 B;
//   - the line of the disk says it: `…, 6 files of the boot and of the faces left out (no glass)`, so a disk smaller
//     than the sum of its folders is a thing a person can explain without reading this file;
//   - **the message of a disk that does not fit no longer gives advice that cannot be taken**: with apps it says to
//     carry fewer, and with none it says that what does not fit is the system's own content —a bigger partition or less
//     `data/`—, which is the sentence the ATOM's build needed.
// The four boards with a glass are untouched: their identity says `LUNOK_GLASS 1` and their image carries the pictures
// and the faces as before (docs/70, docs/85).

// 0.179.12 (11/11): **the Paper's image carries its apps again**. On the board, `run home_eink` answered `no such app:
// home_eink` and the log said `[W][HOME] no app 'home_eink' on this disk; the prompt is yours`. The list of
// `custom_apps` of `[env:m5-paper]` had been emptied the day before, in a commit about the card of the emulator
// (0.178.7) whose subject had nothing to do with it, and the disk image built after it is the system alone: the board
// that comes up with the launcher came up with a prompt, and the comment above the line went on naming the eleven apps
// it no longer asked for.
//   - the list is back —`editor,calculator,about,timeclock,reader,home_eink,shell,luaperf,tz,wipe,fonts`—, which is the
//     same eleven the comment describes and the same eleven a card or `repo install` would have to put back by hand;
//   - **`make_disk` now says it out loud**: it reads `-DLUNOK_HOME_APP` of the environment and, when the boot names an
//     app its disk does not carry, prints `**m5-paper comes up with 'home_eink' and this disk carries it not**: the
//     build is a board with a prompt and a boot nobody can obey`. A warning and not a refusal, because a board with a
//     prompt is a board that works and an empty disk is the normal one for the four boards that carry no app by rule;
//   - the apps live in the image, so a board that lost them gets them back with `-t uploadfs` and not with a firmware
//     upload: the program and the disk are two partitions and one command does not write the other (docs/70, docs/85).

// 0.179.11 (11/11): **four faults a review found, and the one that made a page look dead**. Reading back the work of the
// 0.179.x turned up four, three of them older than the day:
//   - **a key that moved a value painted nothing**, the oldest of the four. `ui_key` handed the arrow to the control, the
//     control changed its value and asked for the draw it owed, and `ui_key` returned without presenting: the cursor of
//     an icon grid, the page of a list, the number of a slider and the day of a calendar moved for nobody unless the app
//     presented by itself, which is what a page waiting inside a `sys.delay` never does. The step now calls
//     `ui_feedback()` before it returns, the fourth place of the 0.98.0 rule (docs/30);
//   - **the fields the system writes are written** (the 0.177.4 went too far). `ui_set_field_bool` and
//     `ui_set_field_number` answered before writing when the value happened to be the default of a field that was still
//     `nil`, so an app reading `editing` inside `on_focus(false)` read `nil` where the system used to hand it `false` —
//     one line of arithmetic on it and the app dies. They now always write and answer `!same`: the answer is about
//     pixels, the field is about state, and they are not the same question;
//   - **the ring that comes back asks for its cell**, the door the 0.179.7 forgot: `ui_ring_shown` invalidated the whole
//     grid, so one key on a grid of kernel cells repainted every picture of the page. It now asks the grid for the one
//     cell, which is the 0.177.3 rule. With the fault put back on purpose: `paint 20,60 300x300`, 90 000 px² for a cell
//     of 100x102 — 10 200 — against `Expected 90000 to be less than or equal to 30000`;
//   - **a joined box takes the pieces it covers out of the map**: `damage_add` merged two regions into one and kept the
//     pieces that were inside the new box, so the map owed a band and also every piece of it. The merge now drops each
//     region contained in the box it forms, with the strongest quality of the ones it joins (the case fills ten by ten
//     with du, one column with gl16 and ten by ten more with du, and the map keeps one `10,10,10,21` at gl16).
// `587 cases` in 24 suites, green in 2 min 51 s, with three new ones — the ring that comes back costs the cell it is on,
// the field an app never wrote reads its default, and a joined box swallows the pieces it covers — and the two breaks
// that turn them red: `90000 to be less than or equal to 30000`, and `a reads 0 nil`. docs/30 and docs/80 say it.

// 0.179.10 (11/11): **an emulator built without the page does not offer a glass either**. The 0.179.9 made a build of the PC
// without `ui.cpp` possible —`PLATFORMIO_BUILD_FLAGS=-DLUNOK_GLASS=0 pio run -e emulator`—, and the first thing it did was
// lie about itself: `--board m5stack-paper` gave a run whose `caps` said `display yes`, whose every drawing command answered
// `no glass on this board`, and whose glass was a buffer nobody would ever paint. The panel of the model is now taken away
// with the page, `kCapDisplay` with it, and the run says why once:
//   - `emulator: this build carries no page (LUNOK_GLASS=0), so m5stack-paper is emulated without its glass: the rest of
//     the board is the model's`, and `caps` answers `display no` and `touch no` because there is nothing to put under a
//     finger. The rest of the model —the keys, the card, the RTC, the battery— is untouched, which is what makes this build
//     worth having: it is the only way to see a board without a glass boot without flashing one (docs/50, docs/80, docs/85);
//   - the glass builds are untouched: the flag is `1` in their identity and the paper emulated there still paints its 2978
//     slots of canvas and its 540x912 view (measured, 11/11);
//   - `584 cases` and the six environments, green.

// 0.179.9 (11/11): **a board with no glass carries neither the page nor the reader nor the faces**. The owner: *«les
// funcions de epub del nucli només han d'estar disponibles si la placa te un eink o pantalla. Tampoc fan falta les fonts en
// aquests dispositius (esp32, koala, m5atom lite i matrix) ni els controls UI o tot lo del UI»*, and later *«Tampoc carrega
// d'imatges... el QR sí, perquè es pot imprimir en text»*. The four boards without a panel were carrying a whole system they
// can never run —and asking for half a megabyte of canvas table at every boot— inside a slot that was nine tenths full:
//   - **the answer is a `#define` in the identity of the board**, `LUNOK_GLASS`, because what it decides is **which
//     translation units exist** and a function cannot leave a file out of a build. `hal/hal.h` includes the identity and
//     `#error`s when a board does not say it, so a new one cannot compile into a system with no page in silence; the
//     question a *running* board answers is still `kCapDisplay` (docs/50);
//   - **eight kernel files are one `#if`**: `ui.cpp`, `font.cpp`, `book.cpp`, `splash.cpp`, `display.cpp`, `bar.cpp`,
//     `touch.cpp` and `orientation.cpp`, and with them `epub`, `zip`, `inflate`, `ttf` and `img` are no longer linked at
//     all —**`qr` stays**, which is the one the owner named, because a QR is printed in text—;
//   - **the bridge loses 64 000 B of bindings**: `ui.*`, the canvas object and its controls, the focus of the dial, the
//     finger, the keyboards, the faces and `epub.*`, and the five tables **do not exist** on those boards (`ui` is `nil`,
//     and a table that could only answer "this build carries no reader" is a promise the build does not keep). `sys`,
//     `fs`, `net`, `json`, `qr`, `button`, `led`, `power` and the rest are untouched (docs/30);
//   - **`kernel/src/no_glass.cpp` is the 189 B that let the shell read the same on both boards**: an `attach` that owes
//     nothing, a bar that says nothing, a finger that is not there, and the two sentences `splash: no glass on this board`
//     and `cfg: this build carries no keyboard` — the commands stay where a person's fingers expect them (docs/50);
//   - **measured, `firmware.bin`**: the ATOM Lite and the DevKit go from **2 302 464 to 2 105 456 B** (−197 008, 8.6 %), the
//     Matrix to 2 105 536 and the T-Koala from 2 378 240 to 2 172 960 (−205 280); the free room in the slot of 2 621 440 B
//     goes from **319 KB to 516 KB**. The Paper grows 208 B —the text of this version and the two new sentences of the
//     shell— and nothing else, because it has the glass (docs/75);
//   - **and it can be watched on the PC**: the emulator's identity says `1` unless the build says otherwise, so
//     `PLATFORMIO_BUILD_FLAGS=-DLUNOK_GLASS=0 pio run -e emulator` is the boot of one of those four boards without
//     flashing a chip —`[I][UI] no glass on this board: no page, no reader and no faces`, `splash` answering `no glass on
//     this board`, `cfg keyboard` answering `none`, `lua` working and `print(2+2)` saying 4— (docs/80, docs/85);
//   - the suite is the glass path and stays green: `584 cases` (`206` of `test_apps`, `105` of `test_ui`, `10` of
//     `test_touch` and `5` of `test_pal`), and the six environments build.

// 0.179.8 (11/11): **a session memory that the system takes is a block the app can see go**. The owner pasted the log of his
// launcher: `[I][MEMORY] home_eink gives back 'apps': 32768 B, the memory is short`, `[W][MEMORY] the memory is short: 1
// block(s), 32768 B given back for an ask of 95296 B`, and then four times
// `present: [string "/apps/home_eink/home_eink.lua"]:248: the session memory was given back`. The relief did what it was told
// to do —a failed ask, nothing else— and the app died of it: the block it was painting from was one of somebody else's, and
// `cache:size()` raised inside every draw of a cell, which is a page of letters and no pictures for the rest of the run:
//   - **the block answers `alive()`** (0.179.7's rule, one door further): true while the object is the block the kernel gave,
//     false once the relief has taken it. Every other call of a block that went still raises —a read of freed memory is
//     worse than a page without a cache—, so the question is asked **before** the use and the app decides (docs/30, docs/75);
//   - **the launcher asks it** where it uses the block —the read of a record, the capture of a picture, the write of the
//     cache, the magic of a resize— and the turn after asks for another block **outside every draw**, with the same steps of
//     the cache, so a board that is still short lands on a block of the names or on none and the page goes on: it says
//     `home: the cache of the session was given back, N app(s) in it` once and rebuilds its records in the same run
//     (`home: cache kept`);
//   - **`test_a_cache_of_the_session_the_memory_takes_does_not_kill_the_page`** is the owner's log made a case: the real
//     launcher of the repository, a block built, `session::release_all()` —the door the relief uses—, a key that paints the
//     page again, and no `present:` line, with the three lines in the order the loop has them (taken, cold, kept). It fails
//     on the launcher of 0.179.7 with **exactly** the line the owner pasted, at line 248, and passes now (`206 cases`, `105`
//     of `test_ui`, `10` of `test_touch` and `5` of `test_pal`, all green).

// 0.179.7 (11/11): **the selector of the focus comes and goes with the hands**. The owner: *«Si s'usa el dit el selector
// del focus ha d'amagar-se. Si es tornen a usar els botons físics ha de tornar. Per defecte el selector de focus ha d'estar
// desactivat fins que s'usen els botons»*. The ring is an ink of the control and it was painted from `focused` alone, so a
// page being read with a finger carried a box saying where the centre key would fall —and it was painted and cleaned on
// every touch for nobody—:
//   - **the bridge keeps the flag of the person, `g_ui_ring_shown`, off by default** and every one of the thirteen paints
//     that read `focused` —the seven that `lua_getfield(…, "focused")` and the five that `ui_field_bool`, plus the
//     `__inverts` a child of a focused box inherits— now asks for **both**. The walk, `ui.focus`, `ui_take_the_focus_from`
//     and the `on_focus` hooks are untouched: `focused` still means the dial is here, it is only not drawn (docs/30);
//   - **a key brings it back and a finger puts it away**, and both paint in the same turn —`ui_ring_shown(on)` invalidates
//     the control the dial is on and the caller asks for the present—, because a ring that waits for the next `present` is a
//     key that answers late and a finger that leaves a ring behind. The flag lives with the machine of the app —`start()`
//     sets it off, next to the slot of the dial and the finger's own numbers—, so **every app opens without a ring** and its
//     first key brings it, which is what "by default" means for a machine that has not seen a key yet;
//   - **the dial never moves with the hand**: after a finger hides the ring the next key walks one control on **from where
//     it was**, which is the case `test_the_selector_of_the_focus_comes_and_goes_with_the_hands` pins with pixels —a page of
//     two buttons, no ink before the first key, the ring on the first and then the second, paper again on both after a tap on
//     empty glass, and the ring back wrapping to the first door with the next key (`205 cases`, `105` of `test_ui`, `10` of
//     `test_touch` and `5` of `test_pal`, all green).

// 0.179.6 (11/11): **the session memory goes only when an ask has failed, and never the block that asked**. The owner, with
// the two lines of the log in front of him: *«Com es decideix si falta ram? Només s'ha de fer si falla una alocació de
// memoria d'una app o sistema. (s'esborra i es reintenta de forma transparent per l'app)»*. He asked how it was decided and
// the answer had two holes in it:
//   - **the rule was already "a failed ask"** in both PALs and not a threshold of free bytes: `allocate` relieves after the
//     two homes have said no and before the same ask is tried again. What the emulator did **not** do is relieve a `resize`
//     that failed in the block's own home —it returned the no from `move_into` and never reached the relief—, so the one
//     ask that most often needs the cache (an app growing its own block) was the one that never got it;
//   - **and the relief could hand back the block the ask was about** (`session::release_all` freed every block, and the
//     `resize` of a session block went on to move the pointer the relief had just given away: a block used after it was
//     free, and a table row lost). Now `pal::set_memory_relief` passes **the block and the bytes of the ask**
//     (`relief(void* keep, size_t bytes)`), `session::release_all(keep, asked)` keeps that one —`[I][MEMORY] home_eink
//     keeps 'apps': 32 768 B, it is the block that asked`— and the warning says what the memory was short for
//     (`… given back for an ask of N B`), which is the half of the sentence that was missing (docs/75);
//   - **two cases hold it**: `test_the_relief_is_a_failed_ask_and_not_a_heap_that_looks_full` —a block that fits runs no
//     relief, the ask that fails runs exactly one with no block to keep and with its bytes— and
//     `test_a_block_that_is_being_moved_is_not_one_the_relief_may_take` —the `keep` of a failed growth, and the block's
//     bytes still there when the answer is no—, plus the table's own case in `test_apps` (`204 cases`, `105` of `test_ui`,
//     `10` of `test_touch` and `5` of `test_pal`, all green).

// 0.179.5 (11/11): **the quality of a paint is the one the change asks for**. The owner, with the code in front of him:
// *«Comptant que totes les apps comencen amb un panell net i un buffer net. Perqué totes refresquen amb tanta qualitat quan no
// fa falta? usa sempre el mode necessari adequat per els canvis del buffer que es volen pujar a l'eink»*. He was right and
// the counter was the worst of it: **every app asked for `gc16` on every page** —four blinks of the glass— over a panel the
// system had already washed before handing it the paper:
//   - **a page is one paint** (0.179.5): half the view or four boxes and the damage goes up whole, because the panel paints
//     one box at a time and every pass waits for its ink —`about` went from five paints for one page to one—; **two boxes are
//     two**, the ring that leaves a control and the one that arrives do not touch (0.177.4). The present of the focus
//     (`present_each`) follows the same rule, so a page built and focused before its first present is one pass and not five;
//   - **and its quality is the one the ink asks for**: `gl16` —sixteen levels and no flash— and `gc16` only when the panel
//     has taken the passes after which it keeps a ghost (`kManyPaints`). **The ghost budget counts passes and not the
//     rectangles a page was drawn with**: forty words are one page and forty passes are forty stains, and counting boxes made
//     every page of text owe the full wash —so did the wash between two apps, which now cleans a page of text with `a2` and a
//     page of grays with `gl16` instead of flashing `gc16` at every app change—;
//   - **a quality an app named is the app's** (`Damage::named`): `ui.rect(…, 'gc16')` is the app saying what its ink needs and
//     a region that names none is a control drawn in `du`, which is the cheap one and not a promise about the panel. The
//     present with a `mode` is untouched: what the app says is what reaches the glass;
//   - **a page of more than eight boxes no longer becomes a `gc16`**: the flood of the damage list merges into one region with
//     the strongest quality it holds, and the present decides what the page goes up with —before this, every page with more
//     than eight controls was a page that blinked, which is most of them—;
//   - **the splash is the one exception the system states**: a boot picture paints the whole glass and its ink never passed
//     through the page, so the first page that covers it is owed whole and clean (`ui::owe_the_page()`, the road a view given
//     back already took). Before this, every launcher said it with a `gc16` on the first line of its page (docs/30, docs/50);
//   - **the apps stop naming a quality they do not know**: `about`, `fonts`, `home_eink`, `luaperf`, `shell`, `timeclock`,
//     `wipe`, the loading page of the reader, its index, its menu and the notice of the shelf present with `ui.present()` and
//     their `ui.clear(15, 'gc16')` is `ui.clear(15)`; a cell of the launcher that arrives is one box and not the grid; the
//     reader keeps `gc16` for a plate because a plate is the one page it knows better than the kernel;
//   - **measured on the emulator**: changing from the launcher to an app is an `a2` of the whole panel (the wash) and a
//     `gl16` of the page instead of two `gc16`s; the launcher's boot with ten apps goes from twenty `gc16` of two boxes to
//     twenty `gl16`; and `test_a_page_goes_up_with_the_quality_its_ink_asks_for` holds it (`204 cases`, `105` of `test_ui`
//     and `10` of `test_touch`, all green).

// 0.179.4 (11/11): **the paginator is the list's and the grid's, and it turns itself**. The owner: *«El Paginador ha d'anar
// integrat amb les llistes o grids, si hi ha mes elements, s'ha de mostrar per si sol a la part d'abaix del control (dins del
// mateix control) s'ha de gestionar el swipe i també si el focus sobrepassa l'últim o el primer item visible per canviar de
// pàgina. Corretgeix les apps que usen paginador manualment. Els controls que tinguin paginació es gestionen ells sols, sense
// intervenció de l'app»*. The foot was already the system's (0.174.2, 0.176.0) and the grid already turned with the finger
// (0.175.0); what was missing was **the rest of the promise**, and `about` had a paginator of its own to make up for it:
//   - **a list turns with the finger too** (0.179.4): forty pixels of a sideways drag are the next page —left— or the page
//     before —right—, **one page per contact and never a press**, and the ring goes to the first row of the page asked for,
//     which is where a grid puts it. `about` read `touch.gesture()` and had a `kSlidePx` of its own; both are gone, and with
//     them the last hand-rolled paginator of the apps (docs/30, docs/90);
//   - **and the page ends where the dial walks out of it**: the rows of the other pages are placed out of the list's frame,
//     where the walk does not reach them, so a step past the last visible row **was** the list leaving the ring and the rows
//     behind the page could only be reached with a finger. Now the step turns the page and the ring lands on the first row of
//     the new one (the last row when the dial walks back), and at the last page the key goes back to the walk and leaves the
//     list, which is the ring of the page and not a dead end (docs/30, docs/90);
//   - **the cursor of a grid is written by the system** (0.179.4): the page of a grid is the page of its `index` and every
//     launcher wrote that number in the `on_focus` of **each** cell —one line per cell, and the app that forgets has a page
//     that never turns—. The ring is the system's and the focus arrives in one place, so `index` is put there, before the
//     ring is painted (a cell of another page is a page the grid lays out again), and `home_eink` loses its `on_focus` and
//     the five lines of comment around it (docs/30, docs/90);
//   - **the ring of a control that is not a stop any more is taken away**: a page turn places the row that had it out of the
//     frame, where `ui_target_index` cannot find it, and a row left saying it has the ring would wear it again the next time
//     its page came back —and its app would never hear `on_focus(self, false)`—. The tree still holds it, so it is found by
//     the slot it had, the road a card already took (0.173.1, docs/30);
//   - **and a cursor that walks within its page paints two cells and not the grid**: the number written by the system goes
//     through the same cost the field of an app goes through (`icon_grid_ring`), because a whole control there is every
//     picture of the page painted for one ring —the case of the launcher measured it at 1 326 000 pixels before, and 200 000
//     is the ceiling of the case (0.177.3, docs/30);
//   - **three cases hold it**: `test_a_finger_that_walks_sideways_over_a_list_turns_the_page`,
//     `test_a_dial_step_past_the_last_row_of_a_page_turns_it` and `test_the_cursor_of_a_grid_follows_the_ring_without_the_app`
//     (`203 cases`, and the suites are green).

// 0.179.3 (11/11): **the memory of a session says every time a block comes and goes**. The owner: *«Afegeix logs per quan un
// app reclama memoria de sessió o es alliberada (tant per l'app com per el sistema)»*, and a cache the system can take away
// without a word is a page that disappears and nobody knows why:
//   - **the ask**: `[I][MEMORY] home_eink asks for 'apps': 32768 B, made` when the block is new, `… and it has them` when it
//     was already there, and `… grown from 4096` when it grew. A refusal says which of the two it was —`and there is no
//     room` or `and the table of 8 is full`— with `[W]`, because the table having no row is not the memory being full;
//   - **the give**: `[I][MEMORY] hog gives back 'hog': 4096 B` when the app gives it (`block:release()`, a door that was in
//     the table of the bridge and had no call in Lua until now), and when it is the **allocator** that takes it —one line per
//     block, with `the memory is short`— plus the one line that sums it up: `[W][MEMORY] the memory is short: 2 block(s),
//     1 081 344 B given back`. The relief is silent when it had nothing to give, so a board that is simply out of memory
//     does not fill the ring with zeros;
//   - **`log_printf` is safe inside the relief**: it formats in a buffer of the stack and writes to the console, and it
//     takes no block from the allocator that is refusing one (docs/30, docs/75);
//   - **measured on the emulator**: the launcher's two runs say `made` and `and it has them`; an app that asks for a megabyte
//     and then eight canvases of two thousand and forty-eight pixels makes the relief fire —`home_eink gives back 'apps': 32 768
//     B, the memory is short`, `hog gives back 'hog': 1 048 576 B, …`, `the memory is short: 2 block(s), 1 081 344 B given
//     back`— and the object of the app that held the block answers `the session memory was given back` from then on; a
//     `block:release()` of the app's own says `hog gives back 'hog': 4 096 B` and the next ask is `made` again.

// 0.179.2 (11/11): **the page of the launcher is up before the door is asked**. The owner: *«vull que l'app home_eink en faci
// us per carregar més ràpid tot al tornar durant la mateixa sessió»*, and the return still walked the door of `apps` before
// the first cell —twenty-one stats of the flash, small but first—:
//   - **a warm block is the page**: the ids and the pictures of the block are the grid, the grid is built and presented
//     before anything of the disk is read, and the log of a return is `cache warm` → `10 cells` → and nothing else;
//   - **and the door is asked in the turn after**, once (`reconcile`): when its list is the page's, that is the whole cost
//     of knowing —no rebuild, no paint— and when an app was installed or removed in this session the page is laid out again
//     with the door's list and only the manifest of the new names is queued. It is the price of a page that does not lie about
//     a disk that changed under it (docs/30, docs/40);
//   - **measured on the emulator**: a return is `home: cache warm, 10 apps, 9 pictures`, `home: 10 cells`, `home: 10 ids: …`
//     and no `home: app`; and with a folder dropped on the card while the board ran, `home: 10 cells` → `home: 11 ids` →
//     `home: 11 cells` → `home: app newone`, which is the reconcile doing its job. `test_apps` 200/200 with the two cases of
//     the launcher unchanged.

// 0.179.1 (11/11): **the cache of the launcher fits the board it is on**. `home_eink` asked for 32 768 B of session memory
// and a board that could not give them had no cache at all, when the records —the manifests, which are what the queue of the
// page pays for one turn each— are 1 544 B and are worth having alone:
//   - **the block is asked for in pieces** (32 768, 24 576, 16 384, 8 192 and 1 544) and the first that the kernel gives is
//     the one the page keeps, so a crowd of apps or a memory that is already spent costs the pictures and not the cache;
//   - **and a picture is only painted from the block when the block has the room for that slot** `kCachePictures + (slot +
//     1) * picture_bytes()`: a block that holds the records and half the pictures is not a block that paints a zeroed box as
//     if it were an icon (docs/30, docs/75);
//   - **measured**: with the block the whole page is still warm in the first turn —`home: cache warm, 10 apps, 9 pictures`
//     and no `home: app`— and the meters of the return `stats reset` … `run home_eink` … `stats` say **three reads of the
//     flash and twenty-one stats**, against **twenty-four reads and sixty-four stats** of the cold page: the twenty-one are
//     the walk of the door of `apps`, which is asked on purpose because an app installed in this session has to appear
//     (docs/30, docs/75).

// 0.179.0 (11/11): **a memory of the session the kernel keeps for an app, and a launcher that costs no disk the second
// time**. The owner: *«Fes que l'app home_eink tingui un caché per apps i icones … Aquesta memoria "de sessió" la facilita el
// nucli i la manté a menys que falti memoria (es el primer tipus de memoria que s'ha d'esborrar si falta PSRAM) … Les claus
// son per app, no per sistema»*:
//   - **`kernel::session`** is a table of eight blocks named by (app, key): `acquire` answers the block that is there or
//     makes it —zeroed, so an app tells a cold cache from a warm one—, grows it when the app asks for more, and answers
//     nullptr when there is no room. The app is the one the kernel knows is running, so `"apps"` is one block for a launcher
//     and another for the app after it (docs/30, docs/75);
//   - **`pal::set_memory_relief`** is the other half: the kernel installs `session::release_all` and **every allocator of
//     the PAL calls it once before refusing a block** —the host's and the chip's, `resize` included— and tries again. The
//     session memory is the first thing the system gives up because it is the only one it can make again, and a generation
//     that only goes up makes an object of an app that held one answer `the session memory was given back` instead of
//     writing into freed memory;
//   - **`sys.session(key, bytes)`** is the door: `read`, `write`, `clear` and `size`, `nil, E_NOMEM` when there is no room;
//   - **`cv:capture(session [, offset])` and `cv:blit(session, offset, x, y, w, h)`** are the pair that makes a cache of a
//     picture worth having: four bits a pixel with the left one in the high nibble, a copy and not a decode, and the same
//     primitive a page of a book uses for its plates;
//   - **and `home_eink` is the first app that uses them**: a block of 32 KB under the key `apps` with the manifests (id,
//     title, icon) and the pictures of the cells, so the second page of the launcher —which is every time an app ends— is
//     whole in the first turn: `home: cache warm, 11 apps, 10 pictures` and **no `home: app` in the console**, because the
//     queue of manifests is empty. A dial that walks over a cell paints a copy of a picture it already decoded instead of
//     reading the file and decoding it, and the dump of the warm page is the cold one —1 377 pixels, all of them the clock
//     of the bar—;
//   - **measured**: `test_apps` 200/200 (the new one is
//     `test_the_memory_of_a_session_is_the_apps_and_not_the_systems`: two apps with one key are two blocks, the same app
//     twice is one, a new block is zeroed, a bigger ask grows and keeps, and the relief empties the table and moves the
//     generation) and `test_ui` 105/105; `mem` says `session memory  32 KB in 1/8 block(s)` while the launcher holds it;
//     the emulator's page warm and cold are the same picture.

// 0.178.8 (11/11): **the last word the board drew waits in the RTC memory for the next run**. The owner asked whether the
// seed could be kept somewhere —the RTC of the Paper, for instance— and the answer is the memory the chip keeps alive
// across a reset, which is where `/rtc` already lives:
//   - **sixteen bytes in `RTC_NOINIT_ATTR`**: a magic (`RNG!`), the last word of the generator, a counter of boots and a
//     check, read as garbage the first time the power comes on and therefore looked at with the magic and the check
//     before it is trusted —a block that does not pass them is not mixed and does not stop the boot either—;
//   - **why `NOINIT` and not `DATA`**: an initialised variable is written on every boot and would erase what the boot
//     before it left. `nm` puts it at `0x50001e08`, next to the 7 168 B of `/rtc` (`0x50000201`), and the region does
//     not overflow on any of the five boards;
//   - **what it closes**: two runs of the same board can no longer share a sequence even if the entropy source hands
//     back the same bytes twice, which was the fault the ADC window of 0.174.8 exists for. The word is written on every
//     `random()` —an RTC memory write and not a flash one, so nothing wears— and the boot's seed mixes it in;
//   - **what it does not do, said without varnish**: the RTC domain dies with the power, so a board that has been
//     unplugged still starts from the noise of its own boot. **On the Paper the battery-backed RTC is the BM8563, and
//     that one keeps the hour and not bytes** —its coin cell feeds the clock chip and not the ESP32's RTC domain—, so
//     the seed does not survive a night without a charger there either. A seed that survives that wants the FRAM
//     (256 B, 10¹² cycles, survives the power) or the NVS, and neither is used: the FRAM is documented as memory an app
//     owns, byte for byte (docs/70), and the NVS is flash that a write per boot would wear (docs/50);
//   - **measured**: `m5-paper`, `m5-atom`, `m5-atom-matrix`, `esp32-devkit` and `ttgo-t-koala` build and link, and the
//     emulator is untouched —its numbers come from `pal_host` and `--seed` (docs/85)—.

// 0.178.7 (11/11): **the card of the emulator is a card and not a second image** (the owner: *«a l'emulador vull que
// totes les apps estiguin posades. Treu les apps del SD per ara»*). The apps of the emulator were already on its flash
// disk —`custom_apps_in_disk = yes`, eleven folders linked to `apps/`, since 8/11— and since 0.174.9 the build also left
// a link per app in `emulator/sd/apps`, so the door of `/apps` showed **every app twice**, the card's with the `*` and
// the word `[card]`, and the launcher needed **twenty-one cells in two pages** for eleven apps:
//   - **`custom_card_apps` is gone from the environment and `seed_card` from `tools/make_disk.py`**: the directory that
//     is the slot (`emulator/sd`, the default of `--sd`) is a directory of this machine that a person fills, exactly
//     like the card of a board, and a build that seeds it is a build that owns somebody's data;
//   - **the result is what the door was built to show and not a doubled list**: `apps` answers **eleven names** and the
//     launcher has **ten cells in one page** (it does not list itself), measured on the emulator after the change. The
//     `*` and `[card]` marks stay where they belong —a card that really carries an app, which is `repo install` and a
//     hand—, and the local links are deleted with `emulator/sd/apps` (`emulator/` is state and not in git);
//   - **and the docs say which one it is now**: docs/70, docs/80 and docs/85 keep the record of the two days as a
//     record —the 10/11 seeded the card, the 11/11 took it out— and say that the flash is where this emulator's apps
//     live.

// 0.178.6 (11/11): **with the radio up, the number is the chip's**. The owner asked how a truly random number is made
// on this board, and the honest answer was that `ISystem::random()` was not one: a xorshift32 seeded once, at the boot,
// with the ADC window and the microsecond counter. The seed is real noise and it never repeats, but the sequence after
// it is a linear map —a handful of outputs and the rest of the run is known—, which is fine for picking one of three
// drawings and useless for a key:
//   - **`esp_random()` is documented as a true random number while Wi-Fi or Bluetooth are enabled**, and on this family
//     the entropy is the RF stage: the number is read from the Wi-Fi peripheral's `WDEV_RND_REG` and the register does
//     not refresh with the radio off, which is why the PAL seeds itself with the ADC at the boot (0.174.8, docs/50);
//   - **and now it asks the driver whether the radio is up** (`esp_wifi_get_mode()` different from `WIFI_MODE_NULL`):
//     when it is, `random()` **answers the chip's word** and stirs the mixer's state with it, so the sequence that
//     answers when the radio goes back down is not the boot's one any more; when it is not, the xorshift32 of always.
//     The radio is not turned on for this —it costs tens of milliseconds and milliamps and the system keeps it off when
//     it has nothing to do (docs/75)—, but a session that needed Wi-Fi is a session of true numbers;
//   - **what it is and what it is not, said in the docs**: with the radio up the words are unpredictable and good for a
//     game, a colour or a salt; they are **not** a cryptographic source —there is no entropy estimate and no health of
//     the source— and keys want a certified TRNG or this generator behind a DRBG (docs/50);
//   - **measured**: `m5-paper` and `m5-atom` link and build, and the only callers are the pick of the boot picture and
//     the two words of `math.randomseed`, so the lock of the driver is paid twice per app and not per number.

// 0.178.5 (11/11): **the boot draws one of its three drawings, and the two logos of the file are gone**. The owner asked
// for the splash image to be one of `boot-2.bmp`, `boot-3.bmp` and `boot-5.bmp` at random, and then, seeing a boot that
// painted `boot-alt.png`, for those two PNGs to be out of the boot's list:
//   - **`picture_of(kind)`** is the new question: a moment may carry several pictures named `<word>-<tail>.<ext>` and one
//     of them is drawn **at every show**, out of `ISystem::random()` —the noise window of the chip on the board and the
//     seeded one of the emulator, so `--seed` draws the same picture twice and a test can say what it sees—. `path_of`
//     keeps its meaning, which is the plain name of the moment, and `show`, `available`, `splash` and `cfg boot` ask the
//     new one, because a listing that says `boot.png` while the boot draws `boot-3.bmp` is a listing that lies;
//   - **a name that is another moment's word is that moment's picture**: the first cut matched every `boot-*` and drew
//     `boot-alt.png` on one boot in six, which is the owner's *«boot-alt.png i boot.png no han d'estar a la llista del
//     boot»*. A tail is free —`boot-night.bmp` is a drawing of the boot—, a word is not;
//   - **and `boot.png` and `boot-alt.png` are deleted from the disk**, together with the moment `boot-alt`: the boot is
//     not a second logo, it is the drawings the disk carries, so `kMoments` goes from six to five and `cfg boot` accepts
//     the word `boot` and no other. A store that still says `boot-alt` falls back to `boot` with the warning that path
//     already had, and `tools/make_splash_images.py` stops writing the two logos (the Moon photograph of `as11-44-6667`
//     and its clipart stay in the history: `git show 9dfa038:data/res/splash/boot.png`);
//   - **the tests say the two things that are true and not which one the dice picked**: two `boot-*.bmp` with the same
//     ink and a `boot-alt.bmp` turned over, so the glass says a drawing was read and the picture of the dead word was
//     not; the case of the wait shows two moments (`boot` then `charging`) instead of two logos, and the words of the
//     command are counted as five;
//   - **measured**: six boots drew the three drawings and never a PNG (3, 2, 3, 3, 5, 2), `splash` lists five moments,
//     `cfg boot boot-alt` answers `cfg: no boot picture 'boot-alt'`, and `cfg boot` prints the drawing that would come
//     up now; the disk of the system is 29 files and 1 892 888 B, and the drawings are what keeps the four 4 MB boards
//     over their partition (docs/70).

// 0.178.4 (11/11): **eight families of letters join the system, and the page that shows them was drawing two letters
// where one belonged**. The owner asked for eight OFL families —Literata, Merriweather, Atkinson Hyperlegible,
// OpenDyslexic, Bitter, Source Serif, Inter and IBM Plex Sans— in `/fonts`:
//   - **their place is the disk's `res/fonts/`** (`data/res/fonts/`), which is what the door `/fonts` calls the system's
//     faces and what `ui::boot` reads its own from, so every board with a glass lists them and an app loads one with
//     `ui.load_font('/fonts/literata-latin1.ttf')` and nothing else (docs/30);
//   - **they are subsets, and they have to be**: a face of a file is at most `kFontFileBytes` (128 KB) and it is read
//     into the app's memory whole, while the originals go from 328 KB (Bitter) to 4.6 MB (Merriweather). Each file is
//     cut to the codes of the `-latin1` faces with `tools/ttf_latin1.py --set latin1`, the variable families are pinned at
//     weight 400, and the eight come out at 208 936 B together —21 KB to 34 KB each—, which the Paper's disk does not
//     notice and the four 4 MB boards cannot take anyway (their image is already over, docs/70);
//   - **composites are flattened by the tool and not by the subsetter**: `lib/ttf` answers `kComposite` for a glyph made
//     of pieces, so a face only cut with `pyftsubset` draws the width and no ink for every accented letter —which is most
//     of a Catalan page—. The first pass here did exactly that and the specimen was the proof (docs/30);
//   - **OpenDyslexic is published only as CFF** and this kernel refuses CFF by name, so its outlines were converted to
//     TrueType (`otf2ttf`) before the subset: the one face of the eight that is a format conversion and not only a cut;
//   - **three of the eight reserve their name** —Merriweather, IBM Plex Sans and OpenDyslexic— and section 3 of the OFL
//     forbids a Modified Version to carry it, so those three are renamed **inside the file** to Lunok Serif, Lunok Sans
//     and Lunok Readable; the file names keep the family, and `data/res/fonts/OFL.txt` says all of it —what was changed,
//     where each one comes from and the licence once— beside `LICENCE.txt`, which now says which faces are whose;
//   - **and a new step in the tool, `--fit-line`**: a step of `scale` is a body of nine pixels (`kOutlineBody`) and a line
//     of eleven (`kOutlineLine`), and the baseline comes from the file's `ascent`. OpenDyslexic's ink is **1,86 em** and
//     its ascent 1,30, so it was drawn a whole line below its box and the rows of a page overlapped; the option scales the
//     ink to the line and puts the baseline where the ink says, and all eight land at 1,212 em of ink in the eleven pixels
//     the system gives them, like DejaVu's 1,207 (docs/30);
//   - **and the app `fonts` was writing UTF-8 where the glass reads bytes** (0.127.0's rule, docs/30): the row of accented
//     letters of its specimen is a string of the source file, so the page drew «Ã€ Ã‰ Ã Ã“ Ãš…» in every face —the mojibake
//     was there before these faces and only DejaVu's wider set made it look like letters—. It is `string.char` now, as the
//     calculator writes its signs, and the specimen says «À É Í Ó Ú à è ì ò ù Ç ç · l'hora i el rellotge» in the face it is
//     showing, which is the whole point of the row;
//   - **measured**: the eight open through `ui.load_font` (`fonts: open <name> TrueType <bytes> <glyphs>`), the specimen of
//     each one drawn and read back from a dump, the emulator's disk is 2 320 223 B in 84 files and the Paper's
//     2 318 939 B in 82, both fitting in their partition, and the four 4 MB boards' are 1 916 844 B in 31 files —over the
//     1 441 792 B of `partitions.csv`, which is where they were before the fonts and where the three splash bitmaps keep
//     them (docs/70). **The two logos deleted in 0.178.5 take 24 149 B away from all of them**: 2 296 267 B in 82 files,
//     2 294 790 B in 80 and 1 892 888 B in 29.

